          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How is blood pressure actually measured, and why is more than one reading needed?",
          "answer": "Blood pressure is written as two numbers, both measured in millimeters of mercury (mm Hg). The **systolic** pressure is the higher number. It is the pressure in the arteries when the heart contracts. The **diastolic** pressure is the lower number. It is the pressure between heartbeats. (Source: *Hypertension (high blood pressure)*, general clinical reference.)\n\nThe correct technique matters because a careless reading is a different measurement, not a slightly less accurate one. The guidance describes these conditions: a validated device, a cuff sized to the arm, and the person seated with their back supported and feet flat. The arm should rest supported at heart level. The person should have no caffeine, exercise or smoking in the 30 minutes before, and rest for five minutes first. They should not talk during the reading. An undersized cuff or an unsupported arm can push the reading up by about as much as a whole category. (Source: *Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets*, society guidance.)\n\nSeveral readings are needed because blood pressure changes constantly. It moves with activity, stress, pain, caffeine, sleep and time of day. It is usually lowest during sleep and rises before waking. Hypertension is defined as pressure that stays raised over time, so a diagnosis rests on repeated measurements. A single high reading in a clinic is not a diagnosis by itself. (Source: *Hypertension (high blood pressure)*.)\n\nThe guidance also explains how readings are sorted. For adults, office readings are classed as normal, elevated, stage 1 or stage 2 hypertension. If the systolic and diastolic values fall into different categories, the higher category applies. (Source: *Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets*.)\n\nThe retrieved material did not cover the mechanics of the cuff itself (how it inflates and deflates, or how the device detects the two values), nor how many readings to take or how far apart. For those details, a clinician or a validated home-monitoring guide would be the right source. If you are checking your own readings, bring your readings and your technique to a clinician to discuss.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
